         Figure 1. Conceptual comparison of lyrics-to-song gener-\n                                         a length regulator (LR) into the SongEcho framework. By                   ation, cover song generation, and singing voice synthesis.\n                                         providing explicit phoneme-level conditioning and precise\n                                         temporal boundaries, MPEcho significantly reduces PER.\n                                         To enable this, we developed Phonsa, a Whisper-based au-                  both paradigms amidst complex accompaniments.\n                                         tomatic transcription model that provides high-precision                      Building upon the lyrics-following capabilities of state-\n                                         phoneme-level annotations for singing voices, overcom-                    of-the-art (SOTA) open-source LTS models such as ACE-\n                                         ing the scarcity of high-quality audio-phoneme pairs. Ex-                 Step [2], Li et al. [1] proposed transforming an LTS model\n                                         perimental results validate the effectiveness of Phonsa for               into a CSG model by incorporating melodic conditioning.\n                                         alignment and MPEcho for end-to-end CSG. The audio                        Specifically, their model, named SongEcho, employs ex-\n                                         samples, code and weights can be accessed from https:                     tracted vocal F0 sequences and voiced/unvoiced (V/UV)\n                                         //lonian6.github.io/MPEcho.github.io/.                                    tags to strengthen the melodic consistency between the\n                                                                                                                   model output and reference song. While representing an\n                     